  "meta": {
    "title": "Rooms from a split footprint, with a circulation net",
    "description": "ROOMS FROM A SPLIT FOOTPRINT: the subdivision of `examples-facade`, with a different stop rule and labels that mean something. The grammar is the same one rule, `cell`, in `applyRules`, and the same two nodes inside it: a relabel that stops a cell, and a `splitBox` that halves the rest across its longest span at a fraction in [0.34, 0.66) drawn from `ruleRoll` on the parent. What differs is exactly what a graph should have to state. THE STOP RULE: a plan stops on a bounded floor AREA, not a bounded size, because a long thin room has a small area while its longest wall says otherwise -- a cell stops once x span times z span is 26 or less (or it is nine splits deep, which never binds here: over seeds 0 to 199 every cell has stopped on area within seven). THE AXIS: `longestOf` \"xz\", the floor plane, so the cut is never vertical whatever the slab's height -- the root is 28 x 0.25 x 20 -- and each cut falls across whichever floor axis is longer at that cell, which is not the same as alternating. THE LABELS ARE THE ROOM KINDS. The stop relabel does not mark a cell finished with a generic label; it writes the kind straight into `kind`, the attribute applyRules dispatches on (`labelAttr` \"kind\" on both nodes): `setAttribute` with values [\"cell\", \"closet\", \"room\", \"hall\"] and the index select(stopped, 1 + ge(area, 13) + ge(area, 19), 0). A cell still being split keeps `cell`, the one label with a rule; a finished one gets closet, room or hall by how many thresholds its area clears, and since none of those has a rule it leaves on `out` carrying it. Summing 0/1 predicates is how a staircase of thresholds is written in the field grammar, which has no switch. The column is a real string attribute: `byAttribute` can dispatch on it, `filterByAttribute` can cut on it, and `spawnInstances` could send each kind to a different asset. THE BANDS MUST SIT INSIDE THE STOP RULE'S RANGE: a cell stops splitting at an area of 26 or less, so a threshold above 26 could never fire and every room would come back the same kind. A child keeps at least 0.34 of a parent larger than 26, so the rooms land between 8.95 and 26.00 square units over seeds 0 to 199 -- 29 to 36 rooms a plan -- and the bands at 13 and 19 are chosen inside that. The rooms tile their footprint exactly, 560 square units of leaves over a 560 square unit root, because `splitBox` resolves the cut on the parent and both halves read it. DOORS ARE A PROXIMITY GRAPH, AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS ADJACENCY -- said plainly because the picture will not say it. `connectPoints` in `relativeNeighborhood` mode over the room centres keeps a pair only when no third centre is closer to both, which thins the complete graph into a circulation-looking net that still contains a spanning tree, so the plan is connected. But two rooms sharing a wall are not always each other's near neighbours, and two rooms that merely pass close diagonally can be joined. A true adjacency test asks whether two boxes share a FACE, and would put one door on each shared wall; this graph does not ask it. Reading it as 'which rooms are next to each other' is reading it wrong; reading it as 'a circulation net over the rooms' is what it is.",
    "tags": [
      "examples",
      "structure",
      "grammar",
      "split",
      "topology"
    ]
  },
